.TH ECR-BILL 1 "2026-11-19" "ecr-bill 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ecr-bill \- estimate Amazon ECR storage cost from describe-images JSON
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
Estimate ECR storage cost from describe-images JSON. Reads files only, no network.

Usage: ecr-bill [OPTIONS] <FILE>...

Arguments:
  <FILE>...  `aws ecr describe-images` JSON files

Options:
      --prices <FILE>
          Price file. The default is the built-in US East (N. Virginia) list
      --as-of <YYYY-MM-DD>
          Date ages are measured to, `YYYY-MM-DD`, midnight UTC. Without it, old images are omitted
      --older-than-days <OLDER_THAN_DAYS>
          An image is old when its push date is more than this many days before `--as-of` [default: 30]
      --untagged-days <UNTAGGED_DAYS>
          Untagged images older than this many days are in the proposed expire rule [default: 14]
      --keep <KEEP>
          Tagged images beyond this count are in the proposed expire rule [default: 10]
      --output <FILE>
          Write the report to this file instead of standard output. An existing regular file is replaced; a symbolic link is refused
      --json
          Print JSON instead of the text table
      --md
          Print Markdown tables instead of the text table. Not with `--json`
      --quiet
          Print only the report and errors: no warning lines on standard error. Not with `--verbose`
      --verbose
          Add progress lines on standard error. Not with `--quiet`
  -h, --help
          Print help
  -V, --version
          Print version

Exit codes: 0 ok, 2 input or usage error, 130 interrupted (SIGINT).
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
ecr-bill reads one or more local files written by
.B aws ecr describe-images
and prices the storage of the images in them, per repository, on this machine.
It lists untagged images and images older than a chosen number of days, and prints a lifecycle policy as JSON text.
It reads files only: it makes no network call, uses no AWS SDK, and does not apply the policy.
The line
.B Not executed
in the report says so.
.PP
Each element of
.B imageDetails
is one image, so the same digest in two files is counted twice.
One GB is 1073741824 bytes.
An image without
.B imageStatus
is priced as ECR Standard.
.B ARCHIVED
images are priced at the archive rate, tiered at 150 TB.
Any other status is not priced, but it still counts as an image and as bytes, and a warning is printed.
Retrieval fees, the 90-day archive minimum, the account free tier, data transfer, managed signing, and encryption are not charged.
.PP
Each repository amount is rounded half away from zero to the cent.
The dollar total is the sum of the unrounded amounts, rounded once to the cent.
The registry id in the input is not printed.
.PP
The amounts are an estimate from list prices, not your AWS invoice: the free tier, credits and discounts are not included.
The text report says so on its second line (\fBEstimate from list prices, not your AWS invoice (free tier, credits, discounts not included)\fR), and every money field is named
.B estimated_usd_month
(in the text lines and in
.BR --json ).
.B --json
also has the top-level field
.B \(dqestimate\(dq: true.
.PP
Without
.BR --as-of ,
storage is priced and the list of old images is omitted; the clock is never used as a default date for the bill.
.PP
If the price date (\fBsource_date\fR) of the price file in use is more than 180 days before
.B --as-of
(or before today without it), one line
.B warning: prices are N days old (price date YYYY-MM-DD, as of YYYY-MM-DD); check current prices
is written to standard error; the exit code and standard output do not change.
A price date that is not written YYYY-MM-DD gives no warning.
.PP
An input file is read as UTF-8, up to 268435456 bytes.
A symlink to a file is followed.
A directory or any other file that is not a regular file is an error.
A path replaced by a fifo between that check and the open is refused the same way, and the open does not wait for a writer.
Warnings are printed as
.B warning:
lines in the text report.
With
.B --json
they are in the
.B warnings
array and are also written to standard error.
.PP
A file holds one JSON value, nested at most 128 levels deep; a second value after it, a duplicate key in an object, or deeper nesting is an error.
A byte count is a whole number from 0 to 18446744073709551615, and a byte total that does not fit in 64 bits is an error.
An image digest must not contain a space or a tab.
A control, line-break, or invisible character in a value is written as
.B \eu{hex}
in the text report and in messages, and as a
.B \euXXXX
JSON escape in
.BR --json .
The same holds for a command-line argument echoed in a usage error, which stays on one line.
.PP
.B imagePushedAt
is a JSON number of seconds since 1970-01-01 UTC, from 0 to 9223372036854775807, or an ISO 8601 string
.B YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS
with an optional fraction and a zone,
.B Z
or
.B +HH:MM
or
.B -HH:MM
up to 23:59.
A missing or null value skips the image for age and gives a warning.
Any other value is
.BR "invalid imagePushedAt" .
.PP
A price file given with
.B --prices
has the shape of
.B prices/ecr-storage.json
in the source tree.
Each rate is a string of digits with at most one decimal point between digits and at most 40 characters, and the decimal type must hold it exactly: at most 28 digits, and nothing above 79228162514264337593543950335.
A field that is absent is reported as missing, and a field of the wrong kind as invalid.
An amount is never rounded before the cent: bytes times a rate divided by 1073741824, a repository's share of a tiered archive amount, and every sum are exact whole-number fractions (512 bits), and the cent is taken once at the end, half away from zero, for each row and for the total.
The error
.B cost does not fit
remains only for a number that would not fit in 512 bits, which no valid input reaches.
.PP
Memory use depends on the shape of the file.
Measured with the release binary, maximum resident set divided by the file size, at 8388608, 16777216 and 33554432 bytes: one-element lists were 103.32, 103.06 and 102.99 times the file, tiny values were 53.07, 53.08 and 53.01 times, and lists nested 120 levels were 152.30, 152.26 and 152.18 times.
The 120-level lists are the highest: 268435456 bytes of that shape would need 40883978240 bytes, calculated from the 8388608-byte run and not run.
100000, 200000 and 400000 ordinary images were 17.72, 17.43 and 17.32 times the file.
The size limit is per file, not per run, and the memory of several files adds up.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --prices \fIFILE\fR
Read the price quote from
.I FILE
instead of the built-in US East (N. Virginia) rates.
The file replaces the whole quote.
An unknown field is ignored, and a duplicate key is an error.
.TP
.B --as-of \fIYYYY-MM-DD\fR
The date ages are measured to, at midnight UTC, from 0000-01-01 to 9999-12-31.
Without it, old images are omitted.
.TP
.B --older-than-days \fIN\fR
An image is old when its push date is more than
.I N
days before
.BR --as-of .
The value is 1 to 36500. The default is 30.
.TP
.B --untagged-days \fIN\fR
Untagged images older than
.I N
days are in the proposed expire rule.
The value is 1 to 36500. The default is 14.
.TP
.B --keep \fIN\fR
Tagged images beyond this count are in the proposed expire rule.
The value is 1 to 10000. The default is 10.
.TP
.B --output \fIFILE\fR
Write the report to this file instead of standard output.
The report is written to a new file in the same directory and renamed over \fIFILE\fR, so \fIFILE\fR holds the old content or the whole new report.
An existing regular file is replaced.
A symbolic link and anything that is not a regular file are refused, and a missing directory is not created.
Any of these, or a write error, is exit status 2 with a message on standard error.
A run that fails, or is interrupted, leaves an existing \fIFILE\fR as it was.
.TP
.B --json
Print one JSON document instead of the text table.
The object starts with
.B "schema_version": 1
and a failed run prints no JSON.
.TP
.B --md
Print Markdown tables instead of the text table.
The amounts are the strings of the text and JSON reports.
A pipe, a backslash and the other Markdown characters in a value get a backslash.
Not with
.BR --json :
the two together are a usage error with exit status 2.
.TP
.B --quiet
Print only the report and errors.
The warning lines on standard error (the price-age warning and, with
.BR --json ,
the image warnings) are not written; they stay in the report.
Standard output and the exit code do not change.
Not with
.BR --verbose :
the two together are a usage error with exit status 2.
.TP
.B --verbose
Write progress lines on standard error: the number of files, each file name (control characters escaped) and
.BR done .
Standard output and the exit code do not change.
Not with
.BR --quiet .
.TP
.B -h, --help
Print the usage text.
.TP
.B -V, --version
Print the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The report was written.
.TP
.B 2
An input or usage error: an unreadable or invalid file, a bad option, or output that cannot be written (a closed, full or broken standard output, or one that is open for reading only; the closed and read-only cases are detected on Linux only).
The message goes to standard error.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted by SIGINT (Ctrl-C), on systems that have that signal.
The message
.B ecr-bill: interrupted by SIGINT
is written to standard error and nothing to standard output.
A signal that arrives once the report is complete and being written is ignored, so a report is never cut in half.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ecr-bill examples/describe-images.json
ecr-bill --as-of 2026-11-09 examples/describe-images.json
ecr-bill --json --older-than-days 90 --keep 20 repo-a.json repo-b.json
ecr-bill --prices my-quote.json images.json
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
README.md, QUALITY.md and SECURITY.md in the source tree.
